    "md5": "e3baeb82bd12cd0d8b59f7708b4397e9",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 2022,
    "chunk_text": "Vesak not on May 1?\n\nThere is a sound that haunts me more than any explosion or cry of protest. It is the silence of Vesak. For the first time in living memory, our streets were empty of pandals. No colourful lanterns danced in the evening breeze. No dansals offer free rice and kindness to strangers. The hurried, joyful footsteps of ordinary people \u2014 driven by devotion and faith \u2014 have vanished. In their place is a nation holding its breath.\n\nThis silence is not peace. It is the sound of deterioration. It is the sound of confusion. And if we do not listen carefully, we will mistake the death of our Dhamma island for a quiet holiday.\n\nLet me speak plainly, without political allegiance but with deep gratitude. Two opposition leaders \u2014 . Namal Rajapaksa and Udaya Gammanpila \u2014 made a decision that should shame every other politician in this country. They chose not to celebrate May Day spectacles. Instead, they gave priority to the Dhamma. In an era where power is everything, they bowed to something higher.\n\nI do not praise them as perfect men. I praise their action. It is a rare lighthouse in a storm of selfishness. Let every party, every minister, every village leader ask themselves: What did I sacrifice for Vesak?\n\nNow, let us move from emotion to evidence. I am a scientist. I deal with numbers that do not lie. Here is the simple, iron-clad calculation of our lunar calendar:\n\n\u00b7 One full moon to the next (Poya cycle) = 29.5 days exactly.\n\n\u00b7 One Buddhist lunar year = 12 such cycles = 12 \u00d7 29.5 = 354 days.\n\nYou do not need a university degree to verify this. You only need a calendar and the courage to look. Yet the government \u2014 a government that has shown again and again its contempt for the five precepts (sila), its addiction to corruption, and its cunning manipulation of everything sacred \u2014 now dares to alter the birth certificate of the Buddha himself.\n\nIf this is true, it is not a policy change. It is a spiritual crime. I struggle to find adequate words for its seriousness."
  },
  {
