    "chunk_size_bytes": 1745,
    "chunk_text": "It certainly does not return to the soil. It does not fund anything as foundational as waste collection. Instead, the burden of this imported, unchecked consumption falls directly onto the earth, transforming the island into a bi-coastal wasteland. In the East, staggering 5x5km trash dumps fester kilometers inland, suffocating the primary dry forests of ancient palu trees. In the South, toxic runoff bleeds directly into the ocean at Habaraduwa. The island is drowning while the treasury swells on its peril.\n\nIf the state is collecting millions, how can it fail to secure a single train crossing?\n\nThe departments of tourism, transport, and public service operate in airtight silos, happily pawning their responsibilities off to the next desk. Nobody cares. Nobody possesses the basic logic, or the moral will, to piece it all together. The only machinery that works flawlessly in this country is the extraction of wealth.\n\nWhile the system extracts from above, the culture erodes from below. The local village ladies\u2014the true stewards who still hold the ancient  Hela-Vedic knowledge of the earth\u2014are being quietly lured into the cold, fluorescent aisles of the very supermarkets the new \u201cwhite walkers\u201d wander through. The sovereign memory that kept these women and their communities alive through decades of brutal civil war is being cheaply traded for plastic-wrapped convenience.\n\nThis tragic forgetting brings into focus a profound truth my friend Karl, an artist, once shared with me: the real war we are waging is against nature. It is a relentless assault against the birds, the bees, and the gracious forest. And until we stop this war against the earth, we will never stop any of the other wars we wage against each other."
