    "md5": "36209eacfdd01b77fd99b0ff8609d3cc",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1701,
    "chunk_text": "\u2018Ah\u2026.are you the one who draws strange kinds of illustrations for the Divaina? Go inside. You can sit. Let me put on a shirt.\u2019\n\nNo one would have thought that this meeting would be the beginning of a unique and fascinating teacher-student relationship.\n\nH A Karunaratne\u2019s work can be described as being creative, colourful, seemingly specific and even rigid, and yet soft and layered with subtext. In his early work one can see the basic features of the art of that time, but also a certain movement abstracted from it all. This is perhaps why he is referred to as the pioneer of abstract art in Sri Lanka.\n\nNevertheless I was quite taken up by his efforts to venture into even more unique areas by techniques such as burning the canvas, use of thread, nails, pieces of metal in the 1990s. He thus stamped himself as the pioneer of contemporary art as well. It is clear that his quiet and solitary efforts generated quite an extraordinary corpus of work. He was not one to stay still in one place like a pond, but moved like a tremendous and deep body of water that challenged the world around him. This itself was quite colourful.\n\nIt is no secret that for many were puzzled by H A Karunaratne\u2019s art. It came with question marks. It generated questions that went unanswered. As for the artist, he was not interested in answering questions. But he did observe the following:\n\n\u2018The connoisseur needs to make an effort and have the necessary interest in order to appreciate a work of art. There should be space and opportunity for anyone to appreciate art in accordance with his or her knowledge and understanding. It is unlikely that everyone could read any of this in the very same way.\u2019"
  },
