    "md5": "09f71941c8d3769fccfab63492f2ef6d",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1747,
    "chunk_text": "So, in this context, one could ask if even his formal students truly understood his teaching techniques. He would penetrate the creation at some point and through it delve into the canvas. The experience obtained in this journey would give him the strength to clear the pathways ahead. This is not easy to understand. It is hard to travel along that path. It is in fact an arduous path.\n\nBuddhist literature recognizes that people are endowed with different kinds and levels of knowledge. It is based on what one knows that one reads the dhamma or any particular subject. The wise teacher offers instruction and offers it in a manner that the student before him can digest. The foundation of H A Karunaratne\u2019s creative world is Buddhist philosophy.\n\nH A Karunaratne was a man of simple ways. He was so soft spoken that it was not easy for someone who sat close to him to hear what he was saying. This is known by everyone who associated with him closely. I have observed on many occasions how he would smile and avoid intense debate. He would even smile if someone ridiculed or poked fun at him. He would offer a short and simple response and that was it. This is not because he couldn\u2019t refute the argument expressed. I know for certain that he had the knowledge, strength and ability to destroy the arguments offered by someone he disagreed with. Such strength, however, he reserved for his creative work.\n\nHe was composed. The discipline developed through exercise served as a filter for his energy and strength which, thereafter, he could pour into the creative exercise. This seems to be what many in Sri Lanka cannot understand. It is the congealed energy filtered from a unique and unforgiving regimen that we find in his creative work."
  },
  {
