    "md5": "3ad052bebd210b94839471e70416671e",
    "chunk_size_bytes": 1446,
    "chunk_text": "First, the credibility of selection must be beyond reproach. Independent panels, guided by clearly articulated criteria and supported by data and performance analytics, are essential to maintaining both fairness and public confidence.\n\nThird, governance structures must continue to evolve. The separation of administration from external influence, strengthened oversight mechanisms, and professional management practices are critical to sustaining long-term stability.\n\nImportantly, a commitment to merit should not be misunderstood as indifference to inclusion. On the contrary, the strongest cricketing systems invest deeply in widening access\u2014across regions, schools, and communities\u2014so that all talented players have the opportunity to emerge. Inclusion at the grassroots strengthens merit at the top; it does not replace it.\n\nSri Lanka\u2019s cricketing identity was built on clarity of purpose and the courage to make principled decisions. Those qualities remain as relevant today as they were in 1996. The challenge is not to choose between tradition and progress, but to recognise that true progress lies in upholding the standards that made success possible in the first place.\n\nIn the final analysis, the equation is straightforward. When systems are credible, opportunities are broad-based, and selection is merit-driven, performance follows.\n\nFor Sri Lanka cricket, reaffirming that balance is not merely desirable\u2014it is essential."
  },
  {
